# Everything `regenerate.sh` produces.
#
# `ledger-settle.ct` and the module it describes are NOT committed. They
# used to be, and the reasoning for committing them was sound as far as it
# went: `boundary_state.json` records the absolute linear-memory address
# `rust-lld` gave the `LEDGER` symbol, so the recording and *that exact
# binary* are one artefact — a module rebuilt by a different toolchain
# diverges at the first host call (measured).
#
# What that argument missed is that producing the two *together* keeps
# them one artefact just as well, and keeps them a description of the
# pipeline that exists rather than of the one that existed when they were
# committed. `scripts/materialize-recording.sh` records both in one run
# into the gitignored `target/test-recordings/`, keyed on this fixture's
# sources and on the content of `ct-instrument` and the browser recorder,
# so either of those moving re-records instead of going unnoticed. The
# 500 KB commit cap that forced the module to be zstd'd no longer applies
# to anything, and `verify.sh` no longer expands a compressed module.
ledger-settle.ct/
module/

# Build output.
wasm-src/target/

# `page/ledger_settle.instrumented.wasm` is what the browser loads, so it
# is written beside the page that serves it. It is only needed to record,
# and `regenerate.sh` builds it in the same run as the recording, so the
# two cannot drift apart. The repo root's blanket `*.wasm` already ignores
# it; this is the statement of intent.
page/*.instrumented.wasm
page/*.instrumented.wasm.manifest.json
